<!-- ============ PAGE 2 ============ -->
<section class="section" id="p2">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section-label">Chapter Two</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">September 11, 2001 — and the War on Terror</h2>

  <div class="img-frame">
    <img src="" data-src="i5" alt="September 11 World Trade Center" loading="lazy">
    <div class="img-caption">The second plane strikes the South Tower of the World Trade Center on September 11, 2001.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>On September 11, 2001, the world changed in a single morning. Terrorists from <span class="kw" data-term="alqaeda">al-Qaeda</span> hijacked four passenger airplanes. Two planes struck the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in New York City. One struck the Pentagon near Washington, D.C. A fourth plane, United Flight 93, crashed in Pennsylvania after passengers and crew fought back. Nearly 3,000 people were killed. The <span class="kw" data-term="911comm">9/11 Commission</span> was later created to produce a full account of the attacks.</p>

    <p>For students, the key term is <span class="kw" data-term="terrorism">terrorism</span>: violence, often against civilians, used to create fear and force political or ideological change. The attacks did not only kill people. They changed airports, government, war, news, policing, immigration, surveillance, and the emotional atmosphere of the entire country.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-frame">
    <img src="" data-src="i14" alt="9/11 close up" loading="lazy">
    <div class="img-caption">The moment of impact at the World Trade Center — the defining image of a generation.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>President Bush declared a <span class="kw" data-term="wot">War on Terror</span>, meaning a broad campaign against al-Qaeda and other terrorist groups, plus governments accused of supporting or sheltering them. In October 2001, the United States and allies invaded <span class="kw" data-term="afghan">Afghanistan</span>, where the <span class="kw" data-term="taliban">Taliban</span> government had sheltered al-Qaeda. Bush said the mission was to remove the Taliban regime and destroy al-Qaeda's base there.</p>

    <p>Congress also passed the <span class="kw" data-term="patriot">USA PATRIOT Act</span> in October 2001. The law expanded government powers in areas such as surveillance, intelligence sharing, terrorism investigation, and law enforcement. Supporters argued that new threats required stronger tools. Critics warned that civil liberties and privacy could be weakened when fear gave government too much reach.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-grid">
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i6" alt="Afghanistan soldiers" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">U.S. troops prepare to board a helicopter in Afghanistan during the War on Terror.</div></div>
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i7" alt="Patriot Act protest" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">A protestor carries a sign at a civil liberties rally, criticizing the expanded surveillance powers of the PATRIOT Act.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The early 2000s therefore became a security decade. The old Cold War fear had been missiles. The new fear was a plane, a backpack, a cell, a border crossing, a hidden network. The enemy was no longer one rival superpower on a map. It was harder to see, harder to define, and harder to defeat.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Key Contrast:</strong> The Cold War (1947–1991) featured a clear enemy — the Soviet Union — on a known map. The War on Terror had no capital city, no uniform army, no borders. This difference shaped every policy decision that followed.</div>

