<!-- ============ PAGE 3 ============ -->
<section class="section" id="p3">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section-label">Chapter Three</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Afghanistan, Iraq, Homeland Security, and Enron</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>After 9/11, the federal government reorganized around protection. In 2002, President Bush signed the <span class="kw" data-term="hsa">Homeland Security Act</span>, creating the <span class="kw" data-term="dhs">Department of Homeland Security</span>. DHS combined all or part of 22 federal agencies into one cabinet department — the largest federal reorganization since the creation of the Defense Department after World War II. Its purpose was to coordinate domestic security, border protection, emergency response, transportation security, and terrorism prevention.</p>

    <p>Then came <span class="kw" data-term="iraq">Iraq</span>. In March 2003, the United States invaded Iraq, arguing that <span class="kw" data-term="saddam">Saddam Hussein</span>'s regime possessed weapons of mass destruction and posed a security threat. Saddam was removed from power, but the major stated weapons stockpiles were not found. The war became long, violent, and controversial, lasting over eight years and killing more than 4,400 Americans and nearly 100,000 Iraqis.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-grid">
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i8" alt="Iraq soldiers" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">U.S. soldiers move through rubble during operations in Iraq, 2003.</div></div>
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i9" alt="Middle East conflict map" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">Map showing nations involved in U.S. military operations in the post-9/11 era.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The Afghanistan and Iraq wars were not the same conflict, though both were tied to the post-9/11 era. Afghanistan was directly connected to al-Qaeda's presence and the Taliban. Iraq was justified through arguments about weapons, dictatorship, terrorism risk, and regional security — but the evidence and reasoning became deeply disputed.</p>

    <p>Meanwhile, Americans were also losing trust in corporations. <span class="kw" data-term="enron">Enron</span>, once a giant energy company, collapsed in 2001 after accounting fraud and hidden debt became public. Executives had cheated investors through false accounting methods. The scandal helped lead to new rules meant to make companies more transparent and executives more accountable.</p>

    <p>The early 2000s were therefore not only about foreign enemies. They were also about hidden danger at home: hidden accounting, hidden risks, hidden intelligence failures, hidden costs. The question became: who can be trusted when the official story keeps changing?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Pattern Recognition:</strong> Notice how the word "hidden" keeps coming up in this era — hidden weapons (Iraq), hidden accounting (Enron), hidden terrorist networks (al-Qaeda). A central theme of the early 2000s is the discovery that official information could not always be trusted.</div>

