<section class="section" id="p4">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section-label">Chapter Four</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Wikipedia, iPod, iTunes, MySpace, and Facebook</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Even as the world became more fearful, digital life grew faster. The <span class="kw" data-term="ps2">PlayStation 2</span> launched in 2000 and became one of the defining game consoles of the era. It was not just a game machine; it could also play DVDs, which helped it become part of the living room entertainment system.</p>

    <p>In January 2001, <span class="kw" data-term="wiki">Wikipedia</span> launched. It was a free online encyclopedia that could be written and edited by volunteers. That sounded chaotic, and sometimes it was. But it also created a new model for shared knowledge: not one printed book on a shelf, but a constantly changing public work that anyone could improve.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-grid">
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i10" alt="iPod nano" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">An iPod nano playing Evanescence — the device that defined portable music for a generation.</div></div>
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i16" alt="Flip phones" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">Flip phones were the dominant mobile devices of the early 2000s, before the smartphone era.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>In October 2001, Apple introduced the <span class="kw" data-term="ipod">iPod</span>, advertising it as a small device that could hold up to 1,000 CD-quality songs in your pocket. Then in 2003, Apple launched the <span class="kw" data-term="itunes">iTunes Music Store</span>, selling digital songs legally online. This mattered because <span class="kw" data-term="napster">Napster</span> had already shown that people wanted downloadable music. Apple helped turn that desire into a commercial system.</p>

    <p>Social life also began moving online. <span class="kw" data-term="myspace">MySpace</span> launched in 2003 and became a major social networking site, especially for music, personal pages, and youth culture. In 2004, <span class="kw" data-term="facebook">Facebook</span> began as a college social network before expanding far beyond campuses.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-frame">
    <img src="" data-src="i11" alt="thefacebook early interface" loading="lazy">
    <div class="img-caption">An early screenshot of "thefacebook" — the site's original interface from 2005, before it became the global platform we know today.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>This was the age when people began to build digital versions of themselves: screen names, profiles, playlists, buddy lists, away messages, favorite bands, photo albums, and public identities. The internet was no longer only a place to search. It was becoming a place to perform the self. The machine was learning people. People were learning the machine.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="img-frame">
    <img src="" data-src="i15" alt="Early 2000s fashion" loading="lazy">
    <div class="img-caption">The early 2000s had a distinctive cultural style — captured here in the era's fashion and celebrity culture.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Then vs. Now:</strong> In 2003, the iTunes Store sold individual songs for 99 cents. Today, streaming services like Spotify give access to tens of millions of songs for a monthly fee. How did the iTunes model change the music industry — and what did it get right and wrong?</div>

