<section class="section" id="p5">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section-label">Chapter Five</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">American Idol, the Human Genome, and Bush Re-Elected</h2>

  <div class="img-grid">
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i12" alt="American Idol judges" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">The original American Idol judging panel: Randy Jackson, Paula Abdul, and Simon Cowell.</div></div>
    <div class="img-frame"><img src="" data-src="i13" alt="Human Genome diagram" loading="lazy"><div class="img-caption">A diagram of the original human genome reference sequence — the product of the Human Genome Project completed in 2003.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The early 2000s also changed celebrity. <span class="kw" data-term="idol">American Idol</span> premiered in 2002 and turned singing auditions into national drama. Viewers did not just watch stars after they became famous. They watched people try to become stars, then voted on the outcome. <span class="kw" data-term="kelly">Kelly Clarkson</span> won the first season, and the show helped define reality competition television for the decade that followed.</p>

    <p>Science, meanwhile, reached one of its great milestones. In 2003, scientists announced the completion of the <span class="kw" data-term="hgp">Human Genome Project</span>. A <span class="kw" data-term="genome">genome</span> is the full set of genetic instructions in a living thing. The project's goal was to produce a reference sequence of human DNA — not a perfect map of every person, but a major guide to the shared human genetic code. The project was completed more than two years ahead of schedule and under budget, a rare victory for a massive scientific collaboration.</p>

    <p>Then came the 2004 election. George W. Bush ran for reelection against Democrat <span class="kw" data-term="kerry">John Kerry</span>. The campaign took place under the shadow of Iraq, terrorism, national security, and arguments over leadership. Bush won reelection, carrying both the Electoral College and the popular vote — a different outcome than 2000.</p>

    <p>So the early 2000s end with two opposing images. One is dark: smoke over Manhattan, war in Afghanistan, war in Iraq, airport security lines, surveillance debates, corporate fraud, and the fear that danger could arrive without warning. The other is bright and electric: Wikipedia pages multiplying, iPods filling with songs, MySpace profiles glowing, <em>American Idol</em> turning strangers into stars, and scientists reading the human genome like an ancient book written inside the body.</p>

    <p>The decade did not simply begin a new century. It shattered one world and built another at the same time.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><strong>The Decade in Two Images:</strong> The burning Twin Towers and the glowing iPod. Both are from the same four-year window. Both changed everything. Understanding the early 2000s means holding both images at once.</div>

